  "notes": "When South gains the lead in hearts, he cannot afford to play on spades. The defense would win the ♠A and cash enough heart tricks to defeat the contract.\n\nSouth must hope to take 2 diamond tricks and 6 club tricks (along with his ♥A). South's best hope is that West has both the ♦K and ♣K. South must manage his single hand entry carefully so that he can take all of his finesses.\n\nIf South plays on clubs first, he may find himself locked in the dummy and unable to take the diamond finesse. South must play on diamonds first to best take advantage of the single hand entry.\n\nIf West ducks South's diamond play, the lead remains in the closed hand to play clubs. South starts by playing the ♣Q. West ducks, but South remains in his hand to take a second finesse in clubs.\n\nIt would have done West no good to cover South's diamond play with the ♦K. South can win the ♦A and cross back to the South hand in diamonds. South can then play clubs as before.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "When South gains the lead in hearts, he cannot afford to play on spades. The defense would win the ♠A and cash enough heart tricks to defeat the contract.",
    "South must hope to take 2 diamond tricks and 6 club tricks (along with his ♥A). South's best hope is that West has both the ♦K and ♣K. South must manage his single hand entry carefully so that he can take all of his finesses.",
    "If South plays on clubs first, he may find himself locked in the dummy and unable to take the diamond finesse. South must play on diamonds first to best take advantage of the single hand entry.",
    "If West ducks South's diamond play, the lead remains in the closed hand to play clubs. South starts by playing the ♣Q. West ducks, but South remains in his hand to take a second finesse in clubs.",
    "It would have done West no good to cover South's diamond play with the ♦K. South can win the ♦A and cross back to the South hand in diamonds. South can then play clubs as before."
  ],
